// case: unsupported-literal-form-in-a-binding
// what: the same literal is a parse error in one position and a wrong number in
//      the other, one token apart, and the wrong number is the one that ships:
//
//        print(0b1010)          Parser error: Expected ')' after arguments
//        val x: int = 0b1010    builds, runs, prints 0. Should be 10, or refused.
//
//      Same for `0o17` (error / 0), `0x1_000` (error / 1) and `1e2` (error / 1).
//      The lexer (`compiler/lexer.orb:253-290`) reads 0x, then digits with `_`,
//      then `.` plus one digit, and stops; the binder takes the integer it has
//      and nothing downstream knows characters were left over. The parse error
//      is the loud half and it is what a reader tests, and its message points at
//      a paren that was never wrong, so the two failures read as unrelated.
//
//      n26 pins the exponent form. This pins the class the differential fuzzer
//      grouped, which is specifically the POSITION asymmetry: 6 of the 12
//      unsupported forms in a binding answer a number, and all 12 of them are
//      refused in a call. `0b1010` is the smallest of them and the most
//      misleading, because 0 is a number a beginner will read as a successful
//      parse of `0` followed by something harmless.
//
//      A fuzzer that only ever puts a literal inside `print(...)` measures half
//      the behaviour of a literal, which is why `int-literal-bind` exists as a
//      category at all. FINDINGS F-0028, and F-0011 for the lexer gap itself.
// known-defect: F-0028
// wrong-value: 0
fn main() -> int {
    val x: int = 0b1010
    print(x)
    return 0
}
